package api

import (
	"net/http"
)

var _ = declareRoute(routeDecl{
	Method:      http.MethodPost,
	Pattern:     "/api/v1/issues/{key}/status-change",
	Auth:        authAny,
	Description: "Write the status of an issue, and with an option what happens to the issues under it or above it, so that each parent's status stays in line with its children's: a parent is done only when each child is done, and while a child is in work its parent is in work. The body is {status, include_children?, unlink_children?, include_parent?, dry_run?, expected_sequence?, actor?}, and expected_sequence and actor are read as PATCH /api/v1/issues/{key} reads them. include_children writes the status on each open issue under the issue, at any depth, that is not in the status's group (not started, in work or done). unlink_children removes each open child from the issue, so that each one becomes a root. include_parent moves each ancestor that is not in work to in_progress. Each issue that an option changes gets a comment from Dispatch that names the call. When the options changed at least one issue, the issue the call names gets one comment from Dispatch too, after the last write, that names the call and lists each of those changes. The call writes one issue at a time, each in a transaction of its own, in an order where each write keeps the rule, and it checks its whole plan before the first write. The answer is 200 {issue, changed}: issue is the issue as the PATCH answers it, and changed has one entry {key, status, parent, previous_last_seq, issue} for each issue whose status or parent the call changed, the issue itself included, in the order of the writes. status and parent are what the call left, previous_last_seq is the issue's last_seq as its own write read it under its lock, and issue is that issue as its write answered it. The answer's issue is read at the issue's own write, so later events of the same call can follow it on the stored issue: a child.status for each direct child that include_children moves after it, for a status in work, and the comment.created of the comment on the issue the call names. So the stored last_seq can be past the answer's, and a client that sends the answer's last_seq as expected_sequence can get 409 ISSUE_CHANGED: it reads the issue again before a write that the sequence fences. With dry_run the call writes nothing and answers the same shape: each entry has the status and the parent that the call would leave and the issue's last_seq now, and no issue. 409 ISSUE_CHANGED: expected_sequence is not the issue's last event. This is decided first, also for a dry run, and again at each write, where an event on the issue that the call did not write stops the call. 403 LEGION_STATUS_SCOPE_REQUIRED, with an insufficient_scope challenge naming legion-status: with DISPATCH_LEGION_STATUS_SCOPE on, a step of the call writes the status of a Legion issue or moves one out from under every labelled issue, and the request's credential holds neither the person scope nor legion-status. The one exception is the owning architect's status write: a broker-issued token whose session the Legion daemon's own token published as the architect of an issue above each issue whose status the call writes, of that issue's own project, before the call and after it, writes done, backlog, icebox or triage, with neither unlink_children nor include_parent, and not done with include_children. This is decided before the first write, also for a dry run, and before PARENT_STATUS_CONFLICT. Each step's write decides it again, so a step whose issue another caller made a Legion issue after the plan stops the call there, after the steps before it. 409 PARENT_STATUS_CONFLICT {code, error, issues: [{key, title, status, relation}], changed}, relation parent or child: the call breaks the rule, with DISPATCH_PARENT_STATUS_RULE on, and for a dry run whatever the setting. 403 BROWSER_SIGN_IN_REQUIRED: include_children with done from a request that presents a token. 403 PROJECT_SCOPE: the request's credential may write one project, and a write of the call is of an issue of another project (a parent may be one), refused before any write, also for a dry run. 403 DOCUMENT_WRITER_NOT_ARCHITECT: a write of the call closes, reopens or takes out of Legion an issue Legion runs, and the session is no person, no architect of that issue or of an issue above it, and not the session of the project's Legion daemon. 400 INVALID_STATUS: the status is not in the lifecycle. 400 STATUS_CHANGE_INPUT: no status, or include_children with unlink_children. 409 PARENT_CONTENDED: the issues of the call moved at each attempt to lock them, and nothing was applied. 409 ISSUE_CLOSED: done on an issue that is closed. 404 NOT_FOUND: no such issue. Each refusal above carries changed: the entries of the writes that committed before it, [] when none did. A repeat under the same Idempotency-Key while the first call runs is 409 KEY_IN_FLIGHT, whose changed is the entries of the writes the first call has committed so far.",
	Handler:     (*server).changeIssueStatus,
	WriteClass:  scopeProject,
	Shape: routeShape{
		Status: http.StatusOK, Response: statusChangeAnswer{}, Request: statusChangeInput{},
		Refusals: []routeAnswer{
			{Status: http.StatusConflict, Body: statusChangeConflict{}},
			{Status: http.StatusConflict, Body: statusChangeRefusal{}},
			{Status: http.StatusForbidden, Body: statusChangeRefusal{}},
		},
	},
})
